            <ArticleTitle>Abdominal and lower-extremity compression decreases symptoms of postural tachycardia syndrome in youth during tilt table testing.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>395-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jpeds.2014.04.014</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0022-3476(14)00342-4</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Little is known about the effectiveness of abdominal and lower-extremity compression in treating postural tachycardia syndrome, particularly in younger patients. This repeated-measures study of 20 young patients with postural tachycardia syndrome demonstrates that compression during head-upright tilt table testing decreases tachycardia (P&lt;.001) and effectively attenuates orthostatic symptoms.</AbstractText>
